    <div class="panel prose" role="tabpanel" id="panH" aria-labelledby="tabH" hidden>
      <h3>Follow the energy</h3>
      <p>Held up high, the phone has potential energy (PE) from its height. As it falls, that PE transfers to kinetic energy (KE). When it lands, the KE transfers into squishing the phone, the case, and the surface. That squish stores PE for a moment. Then the energy leaves as heat and sound, plus a small bounce.</p>
      <h3>Step through the drop</h3>
      <p>After a drop, use the slider under the drop stage to look at any moment, or jump to a key moment such as halfway down or most squished. Choose Pin this moment to keep it, then compare the energy bars of the pinned moments side by side. The squish and spring-back really take only a few thousandths of a second, so the slider stretches them out to make them easy to see.</p>
      <h3>What sets the peak force</h3>
      <p>The landing squishes a little like a spring. The force grows as the squish grows, until the phone stops. The biggest force is the peak force. A softer landing squishes farther, so the same energy is stopped over a longer distance and the peak force is smaller. In the force graph, the shaded area is the phone's energy.</p>
      <h3>When the glass cracks</h3>
      <p>The screen cracks when the peak force goes past the glass's breaking point. Glass is brittle. It bends and springs back until it cracks, so its elastic limit and its breaking point are almost the same. The floor pushes up on the phone with the same size force that the phone pushes down on the floor.</p>
      <h3>What this model leaves out</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Air resistance. It is very small for a phone.</li>
        <li>Landing on a corner or at a tilt. Every drop here lands flat, screen down.</li>
        <li>Real impacts last only a few thousandths of a second and are not perfectly smooth.</li>
        <li>Real glass, which is never exactly the same twice.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The numbers are realistic but simplified. Use them to compare drops, not to predict your own phone.</p>
      <label class="chip" for="optMath"><input type="checkbox" id="optMath"> Show the math</label>
      <pre class="mathbox" id="mathBox" hidden></pre>
    </div>
